1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
Old Faithful's eruptions.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,380
With the data first, the closest bell curve is this one.

3
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:10,110
Put the bell first, and if closeness works both ways, it stays at one.

4
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:12,500
Or does it move to two, or three?

5
00:00:14,333 --> 00:00:18,783
The D stands for KL divergence, named for Kullback and Leibler.

6
00:00:18,983 --> 00:00:26,683
Every maximum-likelihood fit is the closest model by it, and it's often called a distance between distributions.

7
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:28,393
So treat it as one.

8
00:00:28,773 --> 00:00:32,173
Data first, this bell is point seven six bits away.

9
00:00:32,293 --> 00:00:40,153
Shift it or stretch it, and that number only grows, so this is bell A: three and a half minutes, give or take one point one.

10
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,280
But a distance reads the same both ways.

11
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:49,770
Put the bell first, and the same two curves are one point seven bits apart, more than twice as far.

12
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:54,760
Only the order changed, so the order must be built into the formula.

13
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,200
To see why, open it up at one real eruption, two minutes long.

14
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:02,150
The data's curve gives that length a height, p.

15
00:01:02,270 --> 00:01:05,220
The bell gives it q, about a quarter as much.

16
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:13,350
Double q twice and you reach p, so log base two of p over q is about two: two doublings, or two bits.

17
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,310
Do that at every length.

18
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:21,370
Then weight each one by how often eruptions last that long, which is p, and add it all up.

19
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:25,730
Where the bell rises above the data, the grey dips pay a little back.

20
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:29,200
That's the divergence: point seven six bits.

21
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,570
Try a bell that ignores the short eruptions and sits on the long hump.

22
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:38,690
At two minutes, it falls short by eighteen and a half doublings.

23
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:45,660
More than a third of all eruptions are short ones like this, so the bill comes to five point eight bits.

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,360
Now keep that bill, and swap the order for a second one.

25
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,510
Every p trades places with q.

26
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:55,010
The bell supplies the lengths, and the data judges them.

27
00:01:55,510 --> 00:01:56,860
Which bell wins now?

28
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:00,100
Take bell B first.

29
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:05,370
It almost never imagines a short eruption, so ignoring them costs it nothing.

30
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:07,100
It pays point six two.

31
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:14,300
Bell A imagines eruptions in the gap and out in the tails, lengths the data almost never shows.

32
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,610
That costs one point seven bits.

33
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:22,170
Now start from A, and let the bell slide downhill on the bell-first bill.

34
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:28,720
As that bill drains, the other one swells, and the bell settles on the long hump, less than half as wide.

35
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,240
Flip the order, and the closest bell moves to three.

36
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:38,173
Maximum likelihood puts the data first, so its fits stretch over every hump.

37
00:02:38,373 --> 00:02:45,613
Variational inference approximates a Bayesian posterior with the model first, so it settles on one hump.

38
00:02:45,966 --> 00:02:52,826
Blei and colleagues note that variational inference generally underestimates posterior variance.

39
00:02:52,976 --> 00:02:57,516
Here, bell B keeps only fifteen percent of the posterior's variance.

40
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:02,060
Settling on one hump needs that gap to be nearly empty.

41
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:09,430
Smooth the data with R's default setting, and the gap fills enough that the best bell-first fit comes out wide.

42
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:16,280
Smoothing chosen by cross-validation keeps the gap nearly empty, and the fit on one hump.

43
00:03:16,966 --> 00:03:19,726
So closeness depends on which curve goes first.

44
00:03:20,326 --> 00:03:23,396
Bell A never misses an eruption that happens.

45
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:26,616
Bell B almost never imagines one that doesn't.

